WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.920
Everyone knows about the Incredible Hulk.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.800
But what about the Sensational She-Hulk?

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.180
Bruce Banner's cousin Jennifer Walters, a.k.a.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.150
She-Hulk, has done everything from kicking
around with Marvel's First Family to flying

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.070
around the galaxy as a space lawyer.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.859
Let's explore the untold truth of She-Hulk.

00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:17.960
Legal legacy

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.869
Considering how successful the Hulk has been
for Marvel, it stands to reason that they'd

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:24.840
want to fill the green giant's world with
some supporting players.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.039
But it actually took the threat of losing
the rights to a lady Hulk that pushed the

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:30.320
publisher into actually making it happen.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.590
With the Incredible Hulk TV series blowing
up in the late '70s, Marvel started to worry

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.520
CBS might try to spin off a female-led series,
just like how The Six Million Dollar Man led

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.720
to the Bionic Woman.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.440
In order to retain control, Marvel beat them
to the punch and created She-Hulk before anyone

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.760
could develop the idea.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.400
Though as female versions of existing characters
go, no one beats MODAM.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.040
Original fourth-wall breaker

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.440
Everyone loves Deadpool, in large part because
the anti-hero cracks jokes and talks directly

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.990
to viewers about how ridiculous comics can
be.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:04.580
But She-Hulk helped pioneer that concept in
the pages of Sensational She-Hulk for 60 issues

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.840
in the 1980s — several years before Wade
Wilson even first showed up.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:13.570
The cover of the first issue actually featured
She-Hulk holding a copy of her previous standalone

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.140
title, telling readers: "This is your second
chance.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.499
If you don't buy my book this time, I'm going
to come to your house and rip up all your

00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:20.499
X-Men."

00:01:20.499 --> 00:01:24.210
Sensational She-Hulk wasn't afraid to get
weird, and often featured She-Hulk talking

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.170
directly to readers as well as her own writers
and artists.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.359
When the series was cancelled with issue #60,
She-Hulk made good on her promise and came

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:33.829
back for the readers' X-Men comics.

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:34.990
Marvel's fashion icon

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.850
John Byrne's Sensational She-Hulk series was
never afraid to try out unusual things, and

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.350
its acclaimed late 1980s run is known for
establishing much of the personality and look

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.710
fans have come to love about Jennifer Walters.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:51.619
Sensational She-Hulk cast the green superheroine
as a fashion icon of sorts, drawing comparisons

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:56.149
to Melanie Griffith, occasionally changing
up her attire four or five times per issue.

00:01:56.149 --> 00:02:00.229
When she wasn't out busting up super villains,
she was wearing classic early-'90s styles,

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:01.990
like stylish dresses and pantsuits.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:06.689
Or, on one particularly strange occasion,
nearly nothing, during four full pages of

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:07.689
jump-roping.

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:11.249
You can practically hear the nerds breathing
heavily when you look at those pages.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.340
“Ohhhh...loneliness and cheeseburgers are
a dangerous mix.”

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:19.620
As if the books weren't unusual enough, readers
only had to turn to the back pages to find

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.680
the most unfortunately-named letters pages
of all time, considering that the comic was

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.459
about a woman with traditionally masculine,
Hulky traits.

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:28.080
That fan mail section?

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.760
It was called She-Mail.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.840
One of Stan Lee's last great creations

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:34.860
“Greetings true believers.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.660
I’m Stan Lee.”

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:41.810
Throughout the 1960s, comics legend Stan Lee
co-created many of the A-list heroes on Marvel's

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:42.810
roster.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.470
He had a hand in Iron Man, Spider-Man, the
Fantastic Four, the Hulk, Black Panther, Hawkeye,

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.140
and the X-Men, just to name a few.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.109
But after that remarkable run, Lee didn't
create a character for more than a decade

00:02:53.109 --> 00:02:54.459
— until She-Hulk.

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:59.450
Lee co-created the character of Jennifer Walters
with John Buscema in 1980, and she made her

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.110
debut in Savage She-Hulk.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.040
She's one of the last truly successful characters
Lee created.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.000
Unless, of course, you count Stripperella.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Which we don't.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.000
And neither should you.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Substitute Thing

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.349
She-Hulk has been a member of several different
teams over the years.

00:03:12.349 --> 00:03:13.680
One of the most interesting?

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.810
The Fantastic Four.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:18.959
Though Marvel's First Family is typically
comprised of Mister Fantastic, the Human Torch,

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:23.090
Invisible Woman, and the Thing, She-Hulk joined
the roster as the resident strongwoman when

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.060
the Thing temporarily left the team.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:30.260
She started holding down the fort in his absence
in Fantastic Four #265.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.160
Even after that first stint came to an end,
she eventually worked with the Future Foundation

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.920
and teamed up more than a few times with the
FF.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:37.920
Mrs. Man-Wolf

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.549
She-Hulk has had love connections with more
than a few heroes and villains across the

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:45.849
Marvel roster, but one of the most interesting
relationships was her brief marriage to John

00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:49.459
Jameson, son of Spider-Man stalwart J. Jonah
Jameson.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:53.239
John has been portrayed as everything from
an astronaut to a Man-Wolf over the decades.

00:03:53.239 --> 00:03:55.260
But the relationship didn't last.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.349
It's true what they say: relationships between
giant, green lawyers and werewolf astronauts

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:01.129
are doomed from the start.

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:02.700
She faced off against Daredevil

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.420
They're the two most prominent lawyers in
the Marvel Universe, and it's crazy to think

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:11.299
it took until 2014 for She-Hulk and Daredevil
to finally face off in the courtroom.

00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:15.579
The two met in a case involving Steve Rogers,
a.k.a. Captain America — who had lost his

00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:17.170
youth and powers.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:21.130
Writer Charles Soule managed the tricky feat
of balancing the dueling attorneys' abilities,

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.260
both super and legal.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.419
Cinematic She-Hulk?

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:28.491
While the character never wound up in the
live action Incredible Hulk TV series, Marvel

00:04:28.491 --> 00:04:31.630
did try to bring her to the silver screen
in the late '80s.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:35.770
The project got far enough along into development
for the studio to shoot some concept photos

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.280
starring Brigitte Nielsen.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.880
It was even announced as an upcoming project
at the Cannes Film Festival in 1991, along

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.950
with a would-be sequel to Dolph Lundgren's
Punisher movie.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.770
Production fell apart due to a lack of interest,
and Marvel's terrible track record in the

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:49.770
90s.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:50.770
As for Nielsen?

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.230
It was certainly an interesting look for She-Hulk:
they basically just threw her into a green

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:55.230
bodysuit.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.590
And you know what they say: life imitates
art.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:01.780
Years after her involvement with the project
ended, Nielsen found herself in a relationship

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.160
not unlike one of She-Hulk's many romantic
misadventures...

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.160
Thanks for watching!

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.550
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.969
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

